In Python, a local variable is defined inside a function and accessible only within that function,
while a global variable is defined outside any function and accessible throughout the entire program.

Understanding Variable Scope in Python
Python variables have different scopes depending on where they are declared. Here's how local and global variables differ:

🔹 Local Variables
•	Defined inside a function
•	Accessible only within that function
•	Created when the function is called and destroyed when it ends

Example:
def greet():
    message = "Hello, world!"  # local variable
    print(message)

greet()
# print(message)  # This would raise an error: NameError

🔹 Global Variables
•	Defined outside any function
•	Accessible inside and outside functions
•	Exists throughout the program’s lifetime

Example:
message = "Hello, world!"  # global variable

def greet():
    print(message)

greet()
print(message)

Variable Name Conflicts
If a local variable has the same name as a global variable, the local one takes precedence inside the function:
x = "global"

def test():
    x = "local"
    print("Inside function:", x)

test()
print("Outside function:", x)
Output:
Inside function: local
Outside function: global

Using the global Keyword
To modify a global variable inside a function, use the global keyword:
count = 0

def increment():
    global count
    count += 1

increment()
print(count)  # Output: 1
Without global, Python treats count as a local variable and will raise an error if you try to modify it.


Key Differences Between Global and Local Variables
Feature	Global Variable	Local Variable

  Feature	                       Global Variable	                                 Local Variable

1.Defined Where	     Outside all functions      	                          Inside a function
2.Scope	             Accessible throughout the entire program	              Accessible only within the function it's defined in
3.Lifetime	         Exists as long as the program runs	                    Created when function is called, destroyed when it ends
4.Modification	     Requires global keyword inside functions	              Can be modified freely within its function
5.Use Case	         Shared data across multiple functions	                Temporary data used only within one function


Example         
# Global variable
count = 0

def increment():
    # Local variable
    message = "Incrementing count"
    global count
    count += 1
    print(message, count)

increment()
print("Final count:", count)
•	count is global and modified using the global keyword.
•	message is local and only accessible inside increment().





